{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/996742-2e-what-makes-a-good-mystery","id":996742,"title":"[2E] What Makes A Good Mystery?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-01T12:19:45-07:00","id":996742,"title":"[2E] What Makes A Good Mystery?","content":"I plan on running a Mage game soon, but I lack experience with how Mysteries should be structured and solved.<br /> <br /> If you could give me examples from fiction, I'be very glad.<br /> <br /> Thanks in advance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T12:27:57-07:00","id":996751,"title":"","content":"I dunno about fiction, but I've got this right here:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/983034-example-mysteries\">Example Mysteries</a><br /> <br /> Thinking about it a bit more, you ever see the Animatrix? There's a scene where kids are exploring an urban environment and there's this place where space is super weird. Be a pretty good Space mystery.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-02T01:07:19-07:00","id":997042,"title":"","content":"Well if you're a fan of podcasts, the entire Welcome to Night Vale series is basically one gigantic mystery wrapped in secrecy and municipally approved scorpions. It's also a brilliant podcast in it's own right too, so I'd highly recommend it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-02T01:39:41-07:00","id":997049,"title":"","content":"Persona 3 is a good example. A city that has an extra hour that only the supernatural can act in. A tower that only appears during this secret hour that is a manifestation of the local populations fears and insecurities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/182-sciurrus","name":"sciurrus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-09T08:41:24-08:00","id":1000813,"title":"","content":"Persona 4 is also a good example. And so is Devil Survivor. They are good &quot;Setting Mysteries&quot;. <br /> Setting Mystery is a Mystery that exists in the background and defines the place you are playing in. You want it to be big, to be deep but not overcomplicated, and most importantly IMHO, a truly great mystery will have a direct gameplay and storytelling impact on the game. In case of Persona games, Mystery *is* source of the gameplay; in Mage, your Local Mystery should make playing a Mage in that place unique and different than playing the same Mage in different setting. Perfectly, it should either introduce a gameplay feature that will show up often, or encourage specific behavior patterns in Mages, rewarding them for acting in that way. Or the reverse, something should be discouraged and bear heavy risk or price when interacted with.<br /> Or all of them at once.<br /> <br /> Imagine for a moment that you have Mage without Paradox. No Abyss. See it? Okay. Then re-add the Paradox, the Abyss, study how existence of Paradox and Abyss interacts with your previously Paradox-Free Mages. Notice questions it introduced. Explore the thought experiment. Then, after you take your notes and feel satisfied with them, look at them with an assumption that<br /> &quot;My Local Mystery is Paradox. Default Mage has no Paradox. How does Paradox work as a Mystery?&quot;<br /> Gameplay-defining? Storytelling-shaping? Experience-making? Analyze it from different angles. You can redo the procedure with different, already preexisting elements of the system, and try to analyze how they work as in-built default Gameplay Defining Mysteries. This is a very useful thing to do before GMing a Mage game, because a) it helps to &quot;grok&quot; the mechanics, b) forces you to focus your gaze on things you might normally not pay enough attention to, c) help you understand why things are like they are in default, and by knowing that and smashing it against your needs and expectations, twink them to your needs.<br /> And, of course, it helps you understand how to implement Mysteries into game as more than a quest or setting detail. <br /> <br /> Example game-elements to experiment upon:<br /> - Watchtowers and Paths<br /> - Access to specific levels of spellcasting <br /> - Existence or non existence of Yantras <br /> - Forms Magical Society takes and it's direct impact on the game<br /> - Existence of Proximi<br /> etc<br /> <br /> Basically, Mage is already a big pile of Mysteries and quite a lot of them are pretty good; examine them, notice that Mystery isn't always just a quest to solve, and make them living and breathing and game-impacting. As for *counterexamples* of what I feel is good, I will actually use example settings in the book. Why? Not because Mysteries are *bad*, but because they are presented in really bad, not-useful manner. Avoid preparing them like in these write ups. Have a clear picture of what you want to do. Make sure you can represent impacts of your Mystery in actual gameplay, storytelling experience. Pretty Mystery that is just pretty and nice to look at is not a good Mystery. Good Mystery will remind you that hanging around this part of the city at night has real consequences that can be felt directly in gameplay. Don't be afraid to homebrew a whole new mechanic for your Mystery. Feel free to create as many Conditions and Tilts related to them as you want. Use the tools that Chronicles of Darkness provide you to make players experience the Mystery. This includes both storytelling and gameplay, mechanics and &quot;fluff&quot;. Telling a player &quot;this place is weird&quot; is good; telling them that and bestowing a Condition upon them might be even better. Or not. Depends on your playstyle. Make it interactive. Make it living. Make it feel like they can poke it, touch it, and try to catch it. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T08:51:11-08:00","id":1001370,"title":"","content":"For smaller, more Quest-Like Mysteries, here are some thoughts.<br /> <br /> First, you always need a Buy-In. A reason for characters to get interested in tackling the Mystery. Game provides you with a lot of tools for that - Arcane XP, Aspirations, Obsessions are all signs of good goal-oriented design - game is constructed in a way that provides you with both both gameplay and story tools to encourage your characters to chase the Mystery. <br /> From your design stand point, you need a way to introduce the Mystery and make it a potential option without revealing too much or shoving it down the throat of your players. This is harder than it sounds. In my case, that's why we really like our homerule that Quiescence doesn't flat out erases Sleeper's memories of magic or rationalize them as not-supernatural; in our games, it just conceals the Supernal Magic as anything else that makes sense to the victim - be it magic, UFO, science or religion. <br /> <br /> So in our case, one of possible ways to introduce a potential Mystery and make this very first step already interactable is to plant seeds about it in Sleeper testimonies. Searching through blogs, stories, accounts and rumors is fun and can be procedural while still providing variety. <br /> In this first phase - let's call it Discovery Phase - you need to also consider a few things. How old is the Mystery? How many people are aware of it? Who else is currently investigating it? What makes people aware of it, what contributes to obfuscating it? Is larger Mage Society aware of it? Does it fall under someone's &quot;territory&quot;? <br /> <br /> After you write these things down, proceed to Investigation Phase. Investigation Phase is basically about poking it with the stick. This is the meat of everything. But, beware! <br /> This Phase is mainly composed of two things - trying to understand the Mystery and dealing with it's whatever effects. Mystery that just lies and patiently waits for you to solve it is not very exciting and doesn't really use potential the game gives you. So it's important to always look at your Mystery in search for untapped potential.<br /> Investigating the Mystery usually means observing it's impact on the world, using both mortal and Magical means. To design it good, you need to really understand what your Mystery is about, and create a clear projection of it in your head - do that and then start writing down as many possible symptoms, consequences and signs of the Mystery as possible. More the better, don't feel obliged to use them all. It might be a good idea to use this material to revisit Introduction Phase design.<br /> <br /> After you have a good picture of how the Mystery impacts the world (never be satsified with just one thing - always try to have multiple impacts, otherwise Mystery will feel flat. If it really just has to do One Very Specific Thing, then make sure to have multiple examples of that Very Specific Thing running around - for example, if all it does is reanimating animals, then have a reanimated beloved pet, reanimated animal who got shot after scaring the neighbours, and reanimated animal who is out for blood. If Mystery does one thing, show that this one thing can mean multiple things depending on context), you are set pretty good. Make sure that you prepared yourself to manifest these interactions using game engine somehow; write down Conditions, prepare monster templates, figure out spell-like effects, and so on. <br /> Again, be very careful of making Mystery just a dormant thing that waits for players to look at it and be understood at their pace. While these are OK from time to time, it's easy to make them the default, and it makes them a little boring. <br /> <br /> What is very important in Investigation Phase is that Mages are...very...good at figuring things out. While Mysteries are hunted exactly because they usually are something that won't be understood simply by casting a Knowing Spell, don't overdo the &quot;trying to understand&quot; phase. Make it gradual. Players should start with basic idea of what the Mystery is, and then expand their understanding by experiencing the Mystery over the adventure. <br /> If your Mystery isn't very spicy by itself (not every Mystery will have symptoms that result in dramatic and exciting scenes), it's a good idea to spice it up using other means - like rival investigation groups. <br /> <br /> Third, final phase is Dealing With It phase. After your players understand the heart of Mystery, or at least get as close to it as possible (not every Mystery is solvable! Sometimes &quot;solving a Mystery&quot; means &quot;ending it so it won't do more harm, understanding it be damned&quot;), you need to have figured out...what next?<br /> Basically, it's good to have a goal. As noted above, &quot;solving a mystery&quot; in classical, murder mystery sense isn't really always applicable to Mage and their understanding of Mysteries. So you need to basically define for yourself what you think &quot;solving this Mystery&quot; should, could be. If it's a Mystery about evil wardrobe that each children and then spit out their twisted shadows to lure in more victims, &quot;closing/destroying the Wardrobe&quot; is a satisfying SOLVED! condition. <br /> ...and of course, this is very fun, you can have multiple possible SOLVED! conditions, and these SOLVED! conditions can be very different and often - contradictory or mutually exclusive.<br /> <br /> Using our Evil Wardrobe example, one way to SOLVE! it is to &quot;destroy it&quot;. However, someone else might decide that &quot;harness it's power and engineer a way to make Wardrobe Children loyal to me&quot; is also a viable SOLVED! condition.<br /> Again, try to think about as many ways to SOLVE! the Mystery as possible. SOLVING! the Mystery always should DO something and CHANGE something. While it's ok to have, from time to time, a Mystery that just ends with &quot;oh, now I get it&quot;, it's not really that great to have it be the case all the time. After all, Mage is all about consequences; if it doesn't have any, it ain't SOLVED!. <br /> Figuring out multiple SOLUTIONS! is also good, because it can give you more ideas about possible motivations and conflicts; again, allied investigation team might suddenly turn into rival team simply because they have a different idea what to do about the Mystery when people finish poking it with magisticks. <br /> <br /> It's also good idea to think about &quot;rewards&quot; that come with SOLUTIONS!, though &quot;consequences&quot; are a better word imho. Still, always try to have something tangible, touchable and potentially manifestable inside rules as a product of SOLVING! the Mystery. While majority of your rewards for finishing a Mystery will be Arcane Beats and Beats from Aspirations/Obsessions related to solving it, don't feel constrained to make it the only possible currency. One SOLUTION! might reward you with more Beats, while other might reward you with a Condition, Merit or other &quot;something&quot;, like an ally or a way to convert children into Wardrobe-Controlled Agents of our Cabal. This is a very large space that often remains untapped and wasted. Don't let it go to waste. <br /> <br /> You should also note that many Mysteries don't &quot;puff&quot; out of existence after being SOLVED!. This is why it's important for them to have some kind of consequence. SOLVED! Mystery will probably remain in the city and keep influencing it, though Mage society might now be aware of what it does and use it (or take measures to minimize it's impact). Before you introduce a Mystery, always think about it's possible lingering effects. If you don't want it to be A Thing That Will Stay, you need to build in a safety measure that will allow you to explode the Mystery after it outstays it's welcome. <br /> <br /> Oh, and it's always good idea to constantly be looking at previous phases after you are done with your current prep session. Often, you will find a way to improve the things that you figured out first using information you figured out later. <br /> <br /> It's also useful to think about Milestones in your Mystery. These are basically major points in the course of dealing with Mystery that signal moving forward. Mysteries sometimes can make feel players helpless and stuck, thus identyfing points that signify their progress and emphasizing them to make them feel accomplished might increase their enjoyment and engagement with Mystery.<br /> Designing around Milestones is useful, but you can also use &quot;:fishing for Milestones&quot; in your notes as a way to have another thing to do with things you already wrote down. <br /> Milestones don't necessarily need to be something you design you adventure around, but it might still be a good idea to figure them out - they are very useful for pacing and sessions-construction.Achieving a Milestone is a great place to end the session and keep people hyped up. A Mystery that is a constant struggle might be fun for some groups and in general fun thing to do from time to time, but from my experience, a lot of people will get tired by being constantly in the role of someone who is always in the dark. While it's genre-appropriate, be careful about it. People like knowing, people like feeling that their actions are not wasted. Balance sense of progression and mysteriousness.<br /> <br /> <br /> *<br /> I know that a lot of this might be contradictory to, say, some of the &quot;mystery&quot; guidelines you can find about RPG games. In fact, I think it might be contradictory with some CoD-specific ones. It's ok. I find them not fitting my style, and my advice is a fruit of that style. I personally enjoy Mysteries and RPG games the most when there is in fact something behind the curtains, actual world to explore and things that are hidden from my sight, but very hard and very in-place. For people like me, &quot;figure it out as you go&quot; or &quot;leave it in flux&quot; is anathema to what we seek and being reminded of these things often kills our immersion and reminds us that we aren't exploring something &quot;real&quot;.<br /> ...so my advice basically boils down to &quot;figure out all the things&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}